WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
API responses should expose only what the caller is authorized to receive.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:13.400
Response minimization means returning the fields needed for the operation, not every field available in a database model or internal object.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:19.180
Response models, serializers, and field filtering can help when they are used intentionally.

4
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:28.560
They reduce accidental exposure of internal identifiers, administrative flags, secrets, relationship data, or sensitive metadata.

5
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.040
Error message design is part of response safety.

6
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:44.700
Detailed errors can be useful during development, but production clients should not receive raw stack traces, token details, database fragments, internal paths, or sensitive business rules.

7
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:52.720
Pagination and bulk exports also need attention because large responses can expose more data than a user interface would normally show.

8
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:59.060
An export is not just a bigger list response; it is often a higher-risk operation.

9
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:06.860
CORS is a browser mechanism that controls which browser-based origins can make cross-origin requests under browser rules.

10
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.440
It is not general API authentication and not a substitute for authorization.

11
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:15.140
Browser clients and machine clients have different trust models.

12
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:21.560
If browser credentials are involved, cookie behavior, token storage, and CSRF concepts matter.

13
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:27.020
Credentialed cross-origin access should be deliberate and limited to expected origins.

14
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.740
Response safety also includes redirects and security headers at a high level.

15
00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:39.400
Redirect targets should be constrained so unchecked input does not send users or clients to unintended destinations.

16
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:45.340
Headers can provide defense in depth for browser-facing APIs, but they do not replace careful response content.

17
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:54.080
The API should consistently ask: who is the caller, what are they allowed to receive, and what could this response reveal beyond the immediate business need.
